{"id": "DfRJulcs-_m", "title": "Video by feartalkies", "description": "A woman was promised to three different men, by her brother, her mother and her father. Then a demon took her. 🏹\n\nLast tale's answer: Vikram said the offering belonged in the thief's hand.\n\n🗡️ The thief: Dhanavati became his wife when her mother poured the wedding water on his hand. The boy was born by his order, so that he would have a son after death. By the old rule Vikram follows, a son born to a man's wife at his command is that man's son. And every coin that made it happen was his.\n\n📖 The scholar: he gave the boy his blood, but he sold that one night for 500 gold coins, paid from the thief's gold. A man who sells himself has no claim on the child.\n\n💍 The king: raising a child would make him a father. But the heap of gold left with the baby was the thief's. Vikram calls it the price for raising him. The king raised the boy on the thief's money.\n\nVikram & Betaal: The Three Grooms.\n\nThe parts we couldn't fit in the reel: her father first met the magician far from home, on a mission for his king. The astrologers were asked to choose the wedding day. The demon had a name, Dhumrasikha, which we left out. And the magician's flight took the father through all the worlds, not only across the heavens.\n\n(From Somadeva's Ocean of Story, the Vetala tales.)\n\nSo whose bride is she? The wise man who found her… the magician who flew her there… or the warrior who fought for her? 👇\n\n#vikrambetaal #indianhorror #ghoststories #indianfolklore #horrorstories", "channel": "feartalkies", "uploader_id": "58803293522", "uploader": "Fear Talkies | Indian Horror", "timestamp": 1793684458, "like_count": 1283, "comment_count": 16, "http_headers": {"User-Agent": "Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/148.0.0.0 Safari/537.36", "Accept": "text/html,application/xhtml+xml,application/xml;q=0.9,*/*;q=0.8", "Accept-Language": "en-us,en;q=0.5", "Sec-Fetch-Mode":